%[text] %[text:anchor:T_bp0] # Estimate Boost PFC Converter Parameters
%[text] This script sizes a single-phase boost power factor correction (PFC) power stage - a diode-bridge rectifier feeding one boost leg. From the AC input range, DC bus, rated power, switching frequency and allowed ripple, it returns the boost inductance and the DC-bus capacitance. It also returns the required device rating and the conduction mode.
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_bpov] ## Overview
%[text] The diode bridge rectifier makes the line voltage unipolar, so one boost leg serves both half cycles. Two bridge diodes are in the conduction path at all times.
%[text] In continuous conduction the boost switch duty follows from volt-second balance, with modulation index $m=V\_{ac,pk} /V\_{out}${"editStyle":"visual"} :
%[text] $\\left|v\_{ac} \\left(t\\right)\\right|=\\left(1-d\\left(t\\right)\\right)V\_{out} \\;\\Longrightarrow \\;d\\left(t\\right)=1-m\\sin \\theta$
%[text] A boost converter cannot buck, so $m\<1${"editStyle":"visual"} is required at the highest line voltage. The script:
%[text] 1. Checks boost feasibility over the AC input range.
%[text] 2. Sizes the boost inductance $L${"editStyle":"visual"} from the worst-case ripple.
%[text] 3. Reports the continuous-conduction-mode (CCM) margin and the power below which conduction goes discontinuous.
%[text] 4. Sizes the DC-bus capacitance $C\_o${"editStyle":"visual"} from the harmonic ripple and hold-up requirements. \
%[text] Additional equations and definitions are provided in the [Annexure](internal:H_bpannex) at the end.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_bprate] ## Specify Converter Rating
%[text] Enter the boost PFC specifications. The current is highest at minimum line voltage. The inductance requirement and the CCM margin are worst at maximum.
boostPFCSpec.minInputVoltage = simscape.Value(207,"V"); % Minimum AC input voltage (RMS)
boostPFCSpec.nomInputVoltage = simscape.Value(230,"V"); % Nominal AC input voltage (RMS)
boostPFCSpec.maxInputVoltage = simscape.Value(264,"V"); % Maximum AC input voltage (RMS)
boostPFCSpec.lineFrequency   = simscape.Value(50,"Hz"); % AC line frequency

boostPFCSpec.outputVoltage    = simscape.Value(400,"V");  % Regulated DC bus voltage
boostPFCSpec.ratedPower       = simscape.Value(1000,"W"); % Rated (maximum) output power
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_bpsw] ## Specify Switching Frequency and Allowed Ripple
%[text] These two set the boost inductance. The ripple fraction is the peak-to-peak ripple as a fraction of the peak line current, at its worst point over the line cycle.
%[text] The ripple fraction $r\_i${"editStyle":"visual"} also fixes the CCM load range. Conduction mode is decided at the line zero crossing, where the shaped current is smallest. At the maximum line voltage this gives $P\_{CCM} /P\_{rated} =2r\_i m${"editStyle":"visual"} .
%[text] The DC-bus ripple fraction is peak-to-peak as a fraction of $V\_{out}${"editStyle":"visual"} , at twice the line frequency.
boostPFCSpec.switchingFrequency = simscape.Value(65e3,"Hz"); % Boost-leg switching frequency
boostPFCSpec.inductorCurrentRippleFraction = 0.30; % Peak-to-peak inductor ripple as a fraction of peak line current
boostPFCSpec.outputVoltageRippleFraction = 0.05; % Peak-to-peak DC-bus ripple at 2*fLine (fraction of Vout)
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_bphold] ## Specify Output Capacitor Hold-Up
%[text] On loss of mains the DC-bus capacitor holds the bus above a floor voltage for a ride-through time at rated power. Set the hold-up time to zero if ride-through is not required.
boostPFCSpec.holdUpTime       = simscape.Value(16e-3,"s"); % Ride-through time at rated power after loss of mains
boostPFCSpec.minHoldUpVoltage = simscape.Value(340,"V");  % Bus voltage floor during hold-up
%[text] The capacitance is the larger of the two requirements. Here the ripple requirement governs.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_bpmarg] ## Specify Design Margins
%[text] The boost margin is how far the DC bus sits above the highest AC peak, and must be above 1. The device voltage margin sets the recommended device rating only.
%[text] The boost switch and boost diode block the DC bus. The bridge diodes block the line peak.
boostPFCSpec.boostVoltageMargin  = 1.05; % Required ratio of Vout to the maximum AC peak
boostPFCSpec.deviceVoltageMargin = 1.30; % Device voltage rating as a multiple of the blocking voltage
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_bppara] ## Specify Parasitic Parameters
%[text] Parasitic resistances affect the device currents and the efficiency, so use datasheet values. The bridge diode forward voltage sets the bridge conduction loss.
%[text] The DC-bus capacitor starts at the regulated bus voltage, skipping the inrush and boost-up transient. A start-up study needs 0 V and an inrush-limiting element.
boostPFCSpec.inductorResistance  = simscape.Value(0.05,"Ohm"); % Boost inductor series resistance
boostPFCSpec.capacitorResistance = simscape.Value(0.01,"Ohm"); % DC-bus capacitor series resistance
boostPFCSpec.initialCapacitorVoltage = boostPFCSpec.outputVoltage; % DC-bus capacitor initial voltage
boostPFCSpec.bridgeDiodeForwardVoltage = simscape.Value(0.7,"V"); % Per-diode drop, two in the path at all times
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_bpest] ## Estimate Boost PFC Parameters
%[text] Compute the boost inductance and the DC-bus capacitance. `DisplayTableFlag` displays the design table below, with every current reported at its worst value over the AC input range. `SelectBoostPFCComponents` gives the device voltage and current ratings.
%[text] `BoostPFCDefaultInputParameters` computes the same values, so the model also opens without this script.
[designTable, boostPFCDesign] = designBoostPFCConverter(boostPFCSpec, ... %[output:group:6416e4fb] %[output:67742149]
    "DisplayTableFlag", true); %[output:group:6416e4fb] %[output:67742149]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_bpripple] ## Verify Inductor Current Ripple and Conduction Mode
%[text] Two plots over a line half cycle, at minimum, nominal and maximum AC input.
%[text] The ripple peaks where $\\left|v\_{ac} \\right|=V\_{out} /2${"editStyle":"visual"} , not at the AC peak, and reaches the same maximum at every input voltage. The ripple fraction is worst at the highest line, where the peak current is smallest.
%[text] The envelope $i\_L \\pm \\Delta I\_L /2${"editStyle":"visual"} gives the core saturation rating from its upper edge and the valley current from its lower edge.
rippleData = plotBoostPFCInductorCurrent(boostPFCDesign); %[output:6393fcbb]
%[text] The load below which the converter is in DCM over the whole AC half cycle is set at the input voltage peak. The load above which it is in CCM over the whole cycle is set at the zero crossing, where the shaped current is smallest. In the figure, the converter is in CCM over the entire cycle in the green region and in DCM over the entire cycle in the red region. Between the two it is discontinuous only around the zero crossings.
plotBoostPFCCCMBoundary(boostPFCDesign); %[output:6936065e]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_bpctrl] ## Specify Controller Parameters
%[text] Tune the average-current-mode controller against the sized power stage. An outer voltage PI produces a power command. Multiplied by the per-unit rectified line voltage, that command is the per-unit current reference, and an inner current loop tracks it.
%[text] The voltage loop is held near 10 Hz, which keeps the 100 Hz bus ripple out of the current reference.
[controllerTable, boostPFCController] = designBoostPFCController(boostPFCDesign, ... %[output:group:44143755] %[output:58820cf0]
    PhaseMargin = 60, ...              % Target phase margin, both loops (deg) %[output:58820cf0]
    CurrentCrossoverRatio = 15, ...    % Current-loop crossover as Fsw/Fcurrentloopcrossover %[output:58820cf0]
    VoltageCrossoverFrequency = 10, ... % Voltage-loop crossover (Hz) %[output:58820cf0]
    IrefPeakMaxPu = 1.3, ...           % Peak current-reference limit (pu of the rated line-current peak) %[output:58820cf0]
    LoadFraction = 1, ...              % Power command as a fraction of rated power %[output:58820cf0]
    PwmStepsPerPeriod = 200, ...       % Fixed-step modulator resolution per carrier period %[output:58820cf0]
    GateVoltageOn = 15, GateVoltageOff = 0, ... % Gate drive levels (V) %[output:58820cf0]
    DisplayTableFlag = true); %[output:group:44143755] %[output:58820cf0]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_bpannex] ## Annexure: Equations and Definitions
%[text] **Boost feasibility and duty.** With $V\_{pk} =\\sqrt{2}V\_{rms}${"editStyle":"visual"} and $m=V\_{pk} /V\_{out}${"editStyle":"visual"} , the bus must satisfy $V\_{out} \\ge \\textrm{boostVoltageMargin}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}V\_{ac,\\max }${"editStyle":"visual"} , and the CCM duty of the boost switch and its minimum are:
%[text] $d\\left(\\theta \\right)=1-m\\sin \\theta \\;,\\quad d\_{\\min } =1-m\\;\\textrm{at}\\;\\theta =\\pi /2$
%[text] **Line currents (unity power factor).** The bridge passes the line current to the single boost inductor, so the inductor carries the rectified line current:
%[text] $I\_{rms} =\\frac{P\_{out} }{V\_{rms} }\\;,\\quad I\_{pk} =\\sqrt{2}I\_{rms} \\;,\\quad i\_L \\left(\\theta \\right)=I\_{pk} \\sin \\theta$
%[text] **Inductor ripple over the line cycle.** The ripple is quadratic in $\\left|v\_{ac} \\right|${"editStyle":"visual"} and therefore peaks at $\\left|v\_{ac} \\right|=V\_{out} /2${"editStyle":"visual"} , not at the AC peak:
%[text] $\\Delta I\_L \\left(\\theta \\right)=\\frac{\\left|v\_{ac} \\right|d}{Lf\_{sw} }\\;\\Longrightarrow \\;\\Delta I\_{L,\\max } =\\frac{V\_{out} }{4Lf\_{sw} }\\;\\;\\left(V\_{pk} \\ge V\_{out} /2\\right)$
%[text] **Boost inductance.** The largest $\\Delta I\_{L,\\max } \\le r\_i I\_{pk}${"editStyle":"visual"} requirement over the AC input range, which lands on the maximum line voltage where $I\_{pk}${"editStyle":"visual"} is smallest:
%[text] $L=\\max\_{V\_{ac} } \\frac{V\_{out} }{4f\_{sw} r\_i I\_{pk} }$
%[text] **Continuous conduction.** Requiring $i\_L \>\\Delta I\_L /2${"editStyle":"visual"} everywhere, the $\\sin \\theta${"editStyle":"visual"} cancels and leaves $I\_{pk} \>V\_{pk} \\left(1-m\\sin \\theta \\right)/\\left(2Lf\_{sw} \\right)${"editStyle":"visual"} . The surviving factor is the duty, so it is largest at the line zero crossing and smallest at the line peak. The zero crossing is therefore the tightest point, and it sets the load at which conduction first goes discontinuous:
%[text] $L\_{CCM} =\\frac{V\_{pk} }{2f\_{sw} I\_{pk} }\\;,\\quad P\_{CCM} =\\frac{V\_{rms}^2 }{2f\_{sw} L}$
%[text] The line peak sets the other end of the same criterion, below which conduction is discontinuous over the whole cycle. Its duty is $1-m${"editStyle":"visual"} , so that load is lower by the same factor:
%[text] $P\_{DCM} =\\left(1-m\\right)\\frac{V\_{rms}^2 }{2f\_{sw} L}=\\left(1-m\\right)P\_{CCM}$
%[text] **The ripple fraction and the CCM range are one degree of freedom.** Substituting the sized $L${"editStyle":"visual"} at the maximum line voltage, which sets both the inductance and the smallest margin, collapses the two CCM results to reciprocal identities containing neither $f\_{sw}${"editStyle":"visual"} , $L${"editStyle":"visual"} nor the power level:
%[text] $\\frac{L}{L\_{CCM} }=\\frac{1}{2r\_i m}\\;,\\quad \\frac{P\_{CCM} }{P\_{rated} }=2r\_i m\\;\\Longrightarrow \\;\\textrm{full}-\\textrm{cycle}\\;\\textrm{CCM}\\;\\textrm{needs}\\;r\_i \<\\frac{1}{2m}$
%[text] That identity is written in terms of $r\_i${"editStyle":"visual"} , which is defined at the ripple maximum $\\left|v\_{ac} \\right|=V\_{out} /2${"editStyle":"visual"} , so it holds only while the line peak reaches that point. The margin itself is computed from $L/L\_{CCM}${"editStyle":"visual"} directly, which is exact at every AC input, including one whose peak stays below $V\_{out} /2${"editStyle":"visual"} .
%[text] **Peak inductor current (core saturation).** Evaluated numerically from the envelope. The at-AC-peak closed form under-predicts the peak at large ripple fractions:
%[text] $I\_{L,pk} \\approx I\_{pk} +\\frac{V\_{pk} \\left(1-m\\right)}{2Lf\_{sw} }\\;\\;\\left(\\textrm{exact}\\;\\textrm{only}\\;\\textrm{for}\\;\\textrm{moderate}\\;\\textrm{ripple}\\right)$
%[text] **DC-bus capacitance.** A single-phase PFC draws $P\_{in} \\left(1-\\cos 2\\omega\_{line} t\\right)${"editStyle":"visual"} against a constant load, so the capacitor absorbs the whole pulsating term. Integrating gives a ripple amplitude $P/\\left(2\\omega\_{line} C\_o V\_{out} \\right)${"editStyle":"visual"} , hence peak-to-peak twice that. With the hold-up requirement from the energy balance, the capacitance is the larger of:
%[text] $C\_{o,\\textrm{ripple}} =\\frac{P\_{out} }{\\omega\_{line} r\_v V\_{out}^2 }\\;,\\quad C\_{o,\\textrm{holdup}} =\\frac{2P\_{out} t\_{\\textrm{hold}} }{V\_{out}^2 -V\_{\\min }^2 }\\;,\\quad I\_{Co,rms} =\\frac{P\_{out} }{\\sqrt{2}V\_{out} }$
%[text] **Boost-leg conduction currents.** Integrating $i\_L =I\_{pk} \\sin \\theta${"editStyle":"visual"} with $d=1-m\\sin \\theta${"editStyle":"visual"} over a line half cycle gives exact closed forms for the boost switch and the boost diode:
%[text] $I\_{Q,rms} =I\_{pk} \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{4m}{3\\pi }}\\;,\\quad I\_{D,rms} =I\_{pk} \\sqrt{\\frac{4m}{3\\pi }}\\;,\\quad I\_{D,avg} =\\frac{I\_{pk} m}{2}=\\frac{P\_{in} }{V\_{out} }$
%[text] Two exact identities make these self-checking: $I\_{Q,rms}^2 +I\_{D,rms}^2 =I\_{L,rms}^2${"editStyle":"visual"} , because the duty splits one current, and $I\_{D,avg} =P\_{out} /V\_{out}${"editStyle":"visual"} , because the boost diode feeds the bus.
%[text] **Bridge diodes.** Each bridge diode conducts the line current for one half cycle and blocks the line peak, never the bus. Two are always in the conduction path, and the mean rectified line current is $2I\_{pk} /\\pi${"editStyle":"visual"} :
%[text] $I\_{rect,avg} =\\frac{I\_{pk} }{\\pi }\\;,\\quad I\_{rect,rms} =\\frac{I\_{pk} }{2}\\;,\\quad P\_{\\textrm{bridge}} =2V\_f \\frac{2I\_{pk} }{\\pi }$
%[text] **Not sized here.** The EMI filter, inrush limiting, the boost diode's reverse recovery and the thermal design are out of scope. A boost leg is one switch and one diode, so there is no dead time to derive; the modulator's minimum on-time guard is a hardware limit taken as given.
%[text] ### Variable Definitions
%[text] $\\begin{array}{l}\nV\_{rms} ,V\_{pk} -\\textrm{AC}\\;\\textrm{input}\\;\\textrm{voltage},\\;\\textrm{RMS}\\;\\textrm{and}\\;\\textrm{peak}\\\\\nV\_{out} -\\textrm{Regulated}\\;\\textrm{DC}\\;\\textrm{bus}\\;\\textrm{voltage}\\\\\nm=V\_{pk} /V\_{out} -\\textrm{Modulation}\\;\\textrm{index}\\;\\left(m\<1\\;\\textrm{required}\\right)\\\\\nd\\left(\\theta \\right)-\\textrm{CCM}\\;\\textrm{duty}\\;\\textrm{of}\\;\\textrm{the}\\;\\textrm{boost}\\;\\textrm{switch}\\\\\n\\theta =\\omega\_{line} t-\\textrm{Line}\\;\\textrm{phase};\\;\\textrm{a}\\;\\textrm{half}\\;\\textrm{cycle}\\;\\textrm{is}\\;\\theta \\in \\left\\lbrack 0,\\pi \\right\\rbrack \\\\\nL-\\textrm{Boost}\\;\\textrm{inductance}\\;\\left(\\textrm{plant}\\;\\textrm{LBoost}\\right)\\\\\nC\_o -\\textrm{DC}-\\textrm{bus}\\;\\textrm{capacitance}\\;\\left(\\textrm{plant}\\;\\textrm{CBoost}\\right)\\\\\nf\_{sw} -\\textrm{Boost}-\\textrm{leg}\\;\\textrm{switching}\\;\\textrm{frequency}\\\\\nf\_{line} ,\\omega\_{line} -\\textrm{Line}\\;\\textrm{frequency}\\;\\textrm{and}\\;\\textrm{its}\\;\\textrm{angular}\\;\\textrm{value}\\\\\nI\_{pk} ,I\_{rms} -\\textrm{Peak}\\;\\textrm{and}\\;\\textrm{RMS}\\;\\textrm{line}\\;\\textrm{current}\\\\\nr\_i -\\textrm{Peak}-\\textrm{to}-\\textrm{peak}\\;\\textrm{inductor}\\;\\textrm{ripple}\\;\\textrm{as}\\;\\textrm{a}\\;\\textrm{fraction}\\;\\textrm{of}\\;I\_{pk} \\\\\nr\_v -\\textrm{Peak}-\\textrm{to}-\\textrm{peak}\\;\\textrm{DC}-\\textrm{bus}\\;\\textrm{ripple}\\;\\textrm{as}\\;\\textrm{a}\\;\\textrm{fraction}\\;\\textrm{of}\\;V\_{out} \\\\\nV\_f -\\textrm{Bridge}\\;\\textrm{diode}\\;\\textrm{forward}\\;\\textrm{voltage}\\;\\left(\\textrm{two}\\;\\textrm{in}\\;\\textrm{the}\\;\\textrm{path}\\right)\n\\end{array}$
%%
